  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 252 PM EDT THU AUG 20 2026\n\nAt the onset of the extended period, the upper air pattern\ncontinues to feature a prominent upper-level ridge centered over\nthe Intermountain West and Four Corners Region. Further east, a\nlarge, neutrally oriented longwave trough is forecast to cover\nportions of the Central and Eastern US. An embedded shortwave\nwithin the longwave trough is modeled to dig out of northwestern\nCanada into the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley over the weekend.\n\nA cold front will cross into eastern Kentucky, likely around or\nafter 00Z Sunday (8 PM EDT Saturday). Isolated to scattered\nshowers and storms are possible ahead of and along this cold\nfront; PoPs reflect this through the afternoon into the early\nevening. Model soundings suggest MLCAPE ranging between 900-1200\nJ/kg at the time of the frontal passage, along with dew points in\nthe upper 60s, and elevated DCAPE around 800-870 J/kg due to dry\nair in the mid-levels. With 0-6km effective bulk shear around 25\nkts, storms could become better organized with potential for\ngusty and erratic winds. The SPC has placed portions of the area\nin a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) which covers nearly all of\neastern Kentucky north and east of a Somerset-to-London line,\nincluding the I-64, KY-15, and US-23 corridors to the WV/VA\nborders. Some models vary on the positioning of the trough, but\ngeneral thinking is storms should taper off across counties\nbordering West Virginia in the late evening.\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds across eastern Kentucky early next\nweek, ushering in a cooler and drier airmass. Dew points lowering\ninto the upper 50s and lower 60s will be a welcome relief from\nrecent conditions. Dry weather remains Sunday through early Monday.\nModel discrepancies are resulting in low, isolated PoPs for Monday\nand Tuesday afternoon. This stems from the GFS being more\nprogressive with a shortwave tracking out of the Central Plains\ninto the Tennessee Valley, bringing low-end rain chances to\nportions of the forecast area. On the other hand, the ECMWF takes\nthe shortwave out of the Central Plains and directs it deeper into\nthe Southeast, leaving the area free and clear of active weather.\nThe NBM has resolved this by placing 15 percent PoPs across the\narea to account for the possibility of the GFS solution.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, multiple shortwaves embedded in the upper-level\nflow look to bring rounds of shower and storm chances to the\narea Wednesday and Thursday.",
